ok today we're going to learn about  energy efficiency we're going to see  what energy efficiency means we're going  to apply it to a real-life example and  we're going to see how to use this  equation here so first of all what is it  what does energy efficiency actually  means what energy efficiency means is  what proportion of the energy supplied  in a particular situation is being being  transferred into a useful form now to  try make sense of this we're going to  have a look at a real-life example so a  real simple example here of just a  battery powered fan so here's just a fan  here and when you switch it on the  blades turn around and it cools it blows  air to cool you down ok so first of all  we need to think about the energy  transfers that are going on in here so  the energy that it starts off with is it  starts off with energy in the chemical  store because we have energy stored in  the batteries now the useful energy  transfer that we're getting is we're  converting energy from the kinetic of  chemical store to the kinetic store  so the kinetic remember means movement  however this is not the only energy  transfer that's happening here we also  have an unwanted energy transfer as well  because we are also transferring energy  into the thermal storm now the reason  for that is whenever we have work which  is done mechanically like the spinning  of these blades  we have frictional forces and those  frictional forces causes a transfer to  the thermal store so the energy is  dissipated to the surroundings so here  we have energy being converted to the  thermal store now in this particular  context it's not useful to have energy  lost the thermal store it would be  preferable be preferential to have all  of that energy converted from the  chemical store to the Kinect  six door but like I said whenever we've  got these moving parts and these  frictional forces we have energy which  is wasted to the thermal storm so now we  need to have a look at how this  particular example relates to this  equation here because efficiency is the  useful energy transferred divided by the  total energy supplied so let's see what  that means now let's imagine that within  this battery we're supplying this fan  with eight thousand joules of energy so  that is the total energy which is being  supplied to this battery-powered fan now  let's imagine that firm will store let's  imagine that about let's imagine 2,000  joules of energy is converted to is it's  basically transferred to the thermal  store so what we're going to do now is  with this information we've got to  figure out what is the efficiency of  this battery-powered fan so the way that  we do it is like this first of all we  need to figure out the useful energy  which has been transferred so if we have  a quick look we can see that we were  supplied with eight thousand joules so  that was my initial energy 2000 of which  has been converted to a transfer to the  thermal store so all I need to do to  find a useful energy using the idea of  conservation of energy  so no energy is lost sorry no energy is  created or destroyed it's just to  convert it from one form to another if  that huh if that is how much it converts  from thermal store the rest of it must  be useful energy ie converted to the  kinetic store so 6,000 joules is useful  so now I've got that information all I  need to do is put it into this equation  so my efficiency is nice and simply the  useful energy transferred now I've  worked on my useful energy transferred  here this is the amount of energy that  gets converted to the kinetic store  which is 6,000  and I need to divide that by the total  energy supplied which is 8,000 joules  remember whenever waiting with energy  the units are always joules so the  answer to this is I did this on a  calculator previously but please always  use a calculator is 0.75 now it's  absolutely fine to you to leave this as  a decimal if you want to or if you  prefer to express it as a percentage you  just need to times it by a hundred so  either of these answers here are  perfectly acceptable answers to show the  energy efficiency of the transfers  within this electric fan the quick check  to make sure you haven't made a mistake  is these this number here should never  be near over 1 if you've left it as a  decimal and this percentage here should  never be over a hundred if you've  expressed it as a percent so that  basically is how energy efficiency works  thank you
 

